  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Crlp/15122/2016 on 9 March, 2022 Author: K. Sreenivasa Reddy Bench: K. Sreenivasa Reddy The Honourable Sri Justice K. Sreenivasa Reddy Criminal Petition No.15122 Of 2016 Order:",
    "facts": "- This Criminal Petition is filed to quash the proceedings in C.C.No.128 of 2010 on the file of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class, Banganapalli, Kurnool District. On analysis, the Public Analyst, vide report dated 06.10.2009, opined that the sample does not conform to the standard of melting point and hence, it is adulterated. It is submitted that the a notice under Section 13 (2) of the Act, 1954 was sent to the petitioners, which was received by them on 02.06.2010, by which time the shelf life of the sample expired. However, the petitioners, on receiving notice under Section 13(2) of the Act, 1954, made an application before the Court to send the second sample for analysis and the learned Magistrate ordered to send the second sample for reanalysis, but the prosecution failed to produce the sample stating that the sample is not traceable. In view of the same, the petitioners could not avail the opportunity of sending the sample for reanalysis to the Central Food Laboratory.",
    "arguments": "The only ground that has been raised by the learned counsel for the petitioners is that the opportunity to avail valuable right of the petitioners to send the sample for second analysis to the Central Food Laboratory after receipt of notice under Section 13(2) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (for short, 'the Act, 1954'), has been defeated by delaying the process of issuance of the said notice. It is submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners that on 26.08.2009 one sample was sent to Public analyst along with Form IV. 4. Learned Special Assistant Public Prosecutor also concurred with the fact that the sample was not sent to the Central Food Laboratory and the petitioners could not avail the opportunity of getting sample tested before the Central Food Laboratory and the notice has been served on them at a belated stage. 5. In Narayana Prasad Sahu v. State of Madhya Pradesh1, the Apex Court held as follows: \"Under sub-section (2) of Section 13, it is mandatory for the Local (Health) Authority to forward a copy of the report of the Public Analyst to the person from whom the CRLP/15122/2016 on 9 March, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111238632/ 1 sample of the food has been taken in such a manner as may be prescribed. This Court in Vijendra {(2020) 15 SCC 763}, held that mere dispatch of the report to the accused is not a sufficient compliance with the requirement of sub-section (2) of Section 13 and the report must be served on the accused.\" 6. Right conferred on the petitioners to send the sample to the Central Food Laboratory is not an empty formality.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "The only ground that has been raised by the learned counsel for the petitioners is that the opportunity to avail valuable right of the petitioners to send the sample for second analysis to the Central Food Laboratory after receipt of notice under Section 13(2) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 (for short, 'the Act, 1954'), has been defeated by delaying the process of issuance of the said notice. It is submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners that on 26.08.2009 one sample was sent to Public analyst along with Form IV. 4. Learned Special Assistant Public Prosecutor also concurred with the fact that the sample was not sent to the Central Food Laboratory and the petitioners could not avail the opportunity of getting sample tested before the Central Food Laboratory and the notice has been served on them at a belated stage.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "5. In Narayana Prasad Sahu v. State of Madhya Pradesh1, the Apex Court held as follows: \"Under sub-section (2) of Section 13, it is mandatory for the Local (Health) Authority to forward a copy of the report of the Public Analyst to the person from whom the CRLP/15122/2016 on 9 March, 2022 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111238632/ 1 sample of the food has been taken in such a manner as may be prescribed. This Court in Vijendra {(2020) 15 SCC 763}, held that mere dispatch of the report to the accused is not a sufficient compliance with the requirement of sub-section (2) of Section 13 and the report must be served on the accused.\" 6. Right conferred on the petitioners to send the sample to the Central Food Laboratory is not an empty formality."
  },
